[[File:Mallows Bay-Potomac River NMS Hibiscus moscheutos blooming.PNG|thumb|''Hibiscus moscheutos'' in the [[Mallows Bay–Potomac River National Marine Sanctuary]].]]
'''''Hibiscus moscheutos''''', the '''rose mallow''', '''swamp rose-mallow''',<ref name=cosewic>COSEWIC 2004. [http://dsp-psd.pwgsc.gc.ca/Collection/CW69-14-417-2005E.pdf COSEWIC assessment and update status report on the swamp rose-mallow Hibiscus moscheutos in Canada.] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20071214175922/http://dsp-psd.pwgsc.gc.ca/Collection/CW69-14-417-2005E.pdf |date=2007-12-14 }} Committee on the Status of Endangered Wildlife in Canada. Ottawa.</ref> '''crimsoneyed rosemallow''',<ref name = usda>{{cite web | url = https://plants.usda.gov/plant-profile/HIMO | title = ''Hibiscus moscheutos'' | publisher = USDA Plants | access-date = 7 December 2023}}</ref>  or '''eastern rosemallow''',<ref name=ns/> is a [[species]] of [[flowering plant]] in the family [[Malvaceae]]. It is a cold-hardy perennial [[wetland]] plant that can grow in large colonies. The hirsute leaves are of variable morphology, but are commonly deltoidal in shape with up to three lobes.<ref name="Lawns"/> It is found in wetlands and along the riverine systems of the eastern [[United States]] from Texas to the Atlantic states, its territory extending northward to southern [[Ontario]].<ref>{{cite book |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=l-UoAQAAIAAJ |title=Audubon: Beyond Birds : Plant Portraits and Conservation Heritage of John James Audubon |author=Ernest Small |publisher=NRC Research Press |year=2009 |page=220|isbn=9780660198941 }}</ref> Subspecies ''lasiocarpos'' is also native to northeastern Mexico, California, and Utah.<ref name = lasiocarpos>{{cite web |title=''Hibiscus moscheutos subsp. lasiocarpos'' (Cav.) O.J.Blanch. |url=https://powo.science.kew.org/taxon/urn:lsid:ipni.org:names:60448391-2 |website=[[Plants of the World Online]] |publisher=9 March 2026 |access-date=9 March 2026}}</ref>

Numerous forms exist in nature. It is a tall plant, with a height of {{convert|1.5–2.5|m|ft}} and flowers up to {{convert|20|cm|in}} across.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Hibiscus moscheutos / common rose mallow |author= |work=Royal Horticultural Society |date= |access-date=6 December 2023 |url= https://www.rhs.org.uk/plants/50176/hibiscus-moscheutos/details}}</ref> Petal colors range from pure white through various pinks to deep red,<ref>{{cite book |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=52fKDwAAQBAJ&pg=PA132 |page=132 |title=Attracting Birds and Butterflies |author=Barbara Ellis |publisher=Houghton Mifflin Harcourt |year=2020|isbn=9780358106425 }}</ref> and most have an eye of deep maroon.<ref name="Lawns">{{cite book |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=mFiucm8ehPgC&pg=PA301 |page=301 |title=Lawns and Gardens: How to Plant and Beautify the Home Lot, the Pleasure Ground and Garden |author=Nils Jonsson-Rose |publisher=Applewood Books |year=2009|isbn=9781429014229 }}</ref> Taxonomic consensus is lacking for the nomenclature of the multiple subspecies. The flowers are borne apically, whereas the related ''[[Hibiscus laevis]]'' carries bud and bloom along the stem.

==Ecology==
It is a larval host for the [[Pyrgus communis|common checkered skipper]], the [[gray hairstreak]], the [[Io moth]], and the [[Eudryas unio|pearly wood nymph]].<ref>The Xerces Society (2016), Gardening for Butterflies: How You Can Attract and Protect Beautiful, Beneficial Insects, Timber Press.</ref> The plant prefers partial to full sun and is a wetland native, utilizing the constantly moist soils that contain [[loam]], [[silt]] and have acidic [[pH]]. Pollinated by bees and self-compatible. The most effective pollinators for this species are [[Bumblebee|bumblebees]] and solitary anthophorid bees (''[[Ptilothrix bombiformis|Ptilithrix bombiformis]]''). Multiple studies conducted in Maryland show that a majority of rosemallow flowers are pollinated by ''Ptilithrix bombiformis''. <ref>{{cite web |title=Hibiscus moscheutos |url=https://www.fs.usda.gov/database/feis/plants/forb/hibmos/all.html |website=USDA |access-date=1 March 2026}}</ref>
Native to wetlands and creek edges in southeastern United States, found abundantly in all of North Carolina. 

== Morphology ==
Tall herbaceous shrub-like, perrenial forb. Produces multiple stems from a large woody rootstock which are about 3-8 ft tall (1.23m) each year. Thrives in full sun where it blooms at full capacity and becomes resistant to disease. Blooming period lasts up to 1 month but individual flowers are short lived. Typically blooms in Summer and early Fall and produces fruits in late Summer, Fall and Winter.<ref>{{cite web |title=Hibiscus moscheutos |url=https://plants.ces.ncsu.edu/plants/hibiscus-moscheutos/ |website=North Carolina Extension Gardener Plant Toolbox |publisher=North Carolina State University |access-date=1 March 2026}}</ref> 

Flowers are perfect and hollyhock-like and in shades of pink, white, red or burgundy and measure up to 8 inches in diameter. 5 spreading petals with red or burgundy central eye and a staminal column that is light yellow to cream color. Leaves are ovate to lanceolate, alternate, greenish gray to green on adaxial surface, abaxial side white and hairy. Flowers mature into oval, short-beaked, 1-1.25 inch seed capsules that contain a ring of seeds inside.

==Subspecies==
Two subspecies are accepted:<ref name = powo/>
*[[Hibiscus moscheutos subsp. lasiocarpos|''Hibiscus moscheutos'' subsp. ''lasiocarpos'']] {{small|(Cav.) O.J.Blanch.}} – central and southern United States, northeastern Mexico, Utah, and California<ref name = lasiocarpos/>
*''Hibiscus moscheutos'' subsp. ''moscheutos'' – central and eastern United States and southeastern Canada (Ontario)

==Uses==
The edible parts of the plant include its leaf buds and young leaves, flowers, immature seed pods (which can be prepared as [[okra]]), seeds, and roots.<ref name="edible-carolinas">{{Cite book |last1=Musselman |first1=Lytton John |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=A4spEAAAQBAJ |title=Edible Wild Plants of the Carolinas: A Forager's Companion |last2=Schafran |first2=Peter W. |date=2021-10-12 |publisher=UNC Press Books |isbn=978-1-4696-6497-2 |language=en}}</ref><ref name="washington-college">{{Cite web |title=Swamp Rose Mallow - Hibiscus moscheutos {{!}} Washington College |url=https://www.washcoll.edu/learn-by-doing/food/plants/malvaceae/hibiscus-moscheutos.php |access-date=2023-08-28 |website=www.washcoll.edu |archive-date=2023-08-28 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230828011023/https://www.washcoll.edu/learn-by-doing/food/plants/malvaceae/hibiscus-moscheutos.php |url-status=dead }}</ref>

The leaves and roots are known to contain mucilage. Tea from boiled leaves has been used to relieve cough, sore throat, and digestive inflammation. These medicinal attributes of the tea have been used to treat dysentery, lung ailments, and urinary infections. An infusion of dried stalks has been used to treat bladder infections. Hibiscus moscheutos flowers have also been used externally to reduce swelling and pain from bruises and insect stings.<ref name="washington-college"/>

==Cultivation==
This is a popular [[garden]] plant. It can be [[plant propagation|propagated]] by seed, or by crown divisions during winter dormancy, and some success can be achieved by hard-wood stem cuttings. Numerous hybrids of the native North American ''Hibiscus'' species have been released by the commercial nursery trade. In cultivation the species or the hybrids can be used in bog gardens or other water features. They are attractive and have wildlife value for nectar-feeders and birds.

===Cultivars===
[[File:Hibiscus moscheutos3.jpg|thumb|right|Corolla]]
Many cold-hardy hibiscus [[cultivar]]s are [[hybrid (biology)|hybrids]] of ''H. moscheutos'', ''[[Hibiscus coccineus|H. coccineus]]'', ''[[Hibiscus laevis|H. laevis]]'', ''[[Hibiscus militaris|H. militaris]]'', ''[[Hibiscus grandiflorus|H. grandiflorus]]'', ''[[Hibiscus dasycalyx|H. dasycalyx]]'', ''[[Hibiscus mutabilis|H. mutabilis]]''. With indeterminate [[genetics|genetic]] contributions from each parent species.<ref name=winters>Winters, H. F. (1970). [https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007%2FBF02860595?LI=true#page-1 Our hardy ''Hibiscus'' species as ornamentals.] ''Economic Botany'' 24(2) 155-64.</ref> Hibiscus breeders do not preclude the possibility of [[self-pollination]]. However, recent research has shown that artificial pollination just after the flower has opened using a high [[pollen]] load will ensure that most of the resulting seeds are from the selected pollen parent. Early hibiscus breeders were likely aware of this and used it to their advantage.<ref name=snow>Snow, A. A., et al. (2000). Effects of sequential pollination on the success of "fast" and "slow" pollen donors in ''Hibiscus moscheutos'' (Malvaceae). ''American Journal of Botany'' 87(11) 1656-59.</ref>

==Conservation==
In [[Canada]] this plant is listed as a Schedule 1 species of special concern under the [[Species at Risk Act]] (SARA).<ref name=cosewic/><ref name=sar>*[http://www.sararegistry.gc.ca/species/speciesDetails_e.cfm?sid=266 Swamp Rose-mallow.] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130610234444/http://www.sararegistry.gc.ca/species/speciesDetails_e.cfm?sid=266 |date=2013-06-10 }} Environment Canada: Species at Risk Public Registry.</ref> This species was previously categorized as a species of special concern in April 1987, in November 2004 its status was re-evaluated and confirmed by the Committee on Status of Endangered Wildlife in Canada ([[Committee on the Status of Endangered Wildlife in Canada|COSEWIC]]). Globally and nationally in the United states, this species is ranked as a common wide spread plant, in New Hampshire it is classified as vulnerable to secure, due to lack of range and decline in populations. The only area in Canada where this species is found is Ontario where it is classified as Vulnerable and is listed as a species of special concern.

